# Typescript Coding Rules

This document defines the coding standards and best practices for Typescript development.

## Core Requirements

### Code Style

- **Ending Semicolons**: Use ending semicolons for all statements
- **Naming Conventions**: Avoid abbreviations for variable and function names unless they are common (i, j, id, config)
- **Blank Lines**: Put a blank line before and after code blocks (if, for, while, try) unless they are the first/last line of a parent block
- **ESM Imports**: Use ESM for all imports
- **TypeScript**: Use TypeScript for all new code

### Type Safety

- **Avoid `any`**: Never use the `any` type - use proper type definitions or `unknown` when type is uncertain
- **Unused Parameters**: Prefix unused parameters with underscore
- **Readonly Members**: Mark class members as readonly if they are never reassigned

### Error Handling

- **Graceful Error Handling**: Handle errors gracefully with try-catch blocks
- **Error Logging**: Always log errors and provide meaningful error messages using loglayer
- **Async Operations**: Use try-catch or handle promise rejections appropriately for async operations

### Code Quality

- **Remove Unused Variables**: Keep code clean by removing unused variables
- **Small Functions**: Keep functions small and focused on a single responsibility
- **Documentation**: Add JSDoc comments for public functions exported by the module
- **Documentation Focus**: When documenting code, focus on explaining the reasoning and business logic rather than just describing what the code does

## Best Practices

### Code Style Examples

#### Proper Semicolon Usage

```typescript
// Good - ending semicolons
const user = {
  id: 1,
  name: 'John Doe',
};

function getUser(id: number): User {
  return users.find((user) => user.id === id);
}

// Bad - missing semicolons
const user = {
  id: 1,
  name: 'John Doe',
};

function getUser(id: number): User {
  return users.find((user) => user.id === id);
}
```

#### Naming Conventions

```typescript
// Good - descriptive names
const userConfiguration = getUserConfig();
const authenticationToken = getAuthToken();
const databaseConnection = createDbConnection();

// Bad - abbreviations (except common ones)
const usrCfg = getUserConfig();
const authTok = getAuthToken();
const dbConn = createDbConnection();

// Good - common abbreviations
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  const id = items[i].id;
  const config = getConfig(id);
}
```

#### Blank Line Rules

```typescript
// Good - blank lines before and after blocks
function processUsers(users: User[]): ProcessedUser[] {
  const processedUsers: ProcessedUser[] = [];

  for (const user of users) {
    if (user.isActive) {
      const processedUser = processUser(user);
      processedUsers.push(processedUser);
    }
  }

  return processedUsers;
}

// Bad - no blank lines
function processUsers(users: User[]): ProcessedUser[] {
  const processedUsers: ProcessedUser[] = [];
  for (const user of users) {
    if (user.isActive) {
      const processedUser = processUser(user);
      processedUsers.push(processedUser);
    }
  }
  return processedUsers;
}
```

#### ESM Imports

```typescript
// Good - ESM imports
import { createUser, updateUser, deleteUser } from './user-service.js';
import type { User, UserConfig } from './types.js';
import { logger } from './logger.js';

// Bad - CommonJS imports
const { createUser, updateUser, deleteUser } = require('./user-service');
const { User, UserConfig } = require('./types');
```

### Type Safety Examples

#### Proper Type Usage

```typescript
// Good - proper type definitions
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  isActive: boolean;
}

function getUser(id: number): User | null {
  return users.find((user) => user.id === id) || null;
}

// Bad - using any
function getUser(id: any): any {
  return users.find((user: any) => user.id === id);
}

// Good - using unknown for uncertain types
function processData(data: unknown): string {
  if (typeof data === 'string') {
    return data.toUpperCase();
  }

  if (typeof data === 'number') {
    return data.toString();
  }

  return 'unknown';
}
```

#### Unused Parameters

```typescript
// Good - underscore prefix for unused parameters
function handleClick(_event: MouseEvent): void {
  console.log('Button clicked');
}

function processUser(user: User, _options?: ProcessOptions): ProcessedUser {
  return {
    id: user.id,
    name: user.name.toUpperCase(),
  };
}

// Bad - unused parameter without underscore
function handleClick(event: MouseEvent): void {
  console.log('Button clicked');
}
```

#### Readonly Members

```typescript
// Good - readonly for immutable members
class UserService {
  readonly baseUrl: string;
  readonly apiVersion: string;

  constructor(baseUrl: string, apiVersion: string) {
    this.baseUrl = baseUrl;
    this.apiVersion = apiVersion;
  }

  getUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
    return fetch(`${this.baseUrl}/api/${this.apiVersion}/users/${id}`);
  }
}
```

### Error Handling Examples

#### Graceful Error Handling with Loglayer

```typescript
// Good - comprehensive error handling with loglayer
async function fetchUserData(userId: number): Promise<UserData> {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText}`);
    }

    const userData = await response.json();
    return userData;
  } catch (error) {
    logger.withError(error).error('Failed to fetch user data');
    logger.withMetadata({ userId }).error('Failed to fetch user data');

    throw new Error(`Failed to fetch user data for user ${userId}`);
  }
}

// Good - promise rejection handling with loglayer
function processUserAsync(userId: number): Promise<void> {
  return fetchUserData(userId)
    .then((userData) => {
      return processUserData(userData);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
      logger.withError(error).error('Failed to process user');
      logger.withMetadata({ userId }).error('Failed to process user');
      throw new Error(`User processing failed for user ${userId}`);
    });
}
```

#### Async Error Handling with Loglayer

```typescript
// Good - async/await with try-catch using loglayer
async function createUser(userData: CreateUserData): Promise<User> {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/users', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      },
      body: JSON.stringify(userData),
    });

    if (!response.ok) {
      const errorData = await response.json();
      throw new Error(errorData.message || 'Failed to create user');
    }

    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    logger.withError(error).error('User creation failed');
    logger.withMetadata({ userData }).error('User creation failed');

    throw new Error('Failed to create user. Please try again.');
  }
}
```

### Code Quality Examples

#### Small, Focused Functions

```typescript
// Good - small, focused functions
function validateEmail(email: string): boolean {
  const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
  return emailRegex.test(email);
}

function sanitizeUserInput(input: string): string {
  return input.trim().replace(/[<>]/g, '');
}

function createUser(userData: CreateUserData): User {
  if (!validateEmail(userData.email)) {
    throw new Error('Invalid email format');
  }

  const sanitizedName = sanitizeUserInput(userData.name);

  return {
    id: generateUserId(),
    name: sanitizedName,
    email: userData.email.toLowerCase(),
    createdAt: new Date(),
  };
}

// Bad - large, unfocused function
function processUser(userData: any): any {
  // 50+ lines of mixed responsibilities
  // Email validation, input sanitization, user creation, logging, etc.
}
```

